# FilRunway configuration.
# Copy to .env.local and fill in before switching to live mode.
# .env.local is gitignored; .env.example is the only env file that may be committed.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MODE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 'mock' (default) runs MockChainAdapter: no keys, no RPC, no funds. The runway
# drains against accelerated wall-clock time so the whole demo is watchable.
# 'live' runs SynapseChainAdapter against Filecoin Calibration for real.
#
# NOTE: 'live' with a missing or malformed FILECOIN_PRIVATE_KEY fails LOUDLY at
# startup. It deliberately does NOT fall back to the mock -- simulated numbers
# under a "LIVE" badge would be worse than an error page.
FILRUNWAY_MODE=mock

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LIVE MODE CREDENTIALS  (required only when FILRUNWAY_MODE=live)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Private key of the agent wallet on Filecoin Calibration. 32-byte hex, 0x
# prefix optional. Read ONLY by src/lib/chain/synapse.ts and scripts/bootstrap.ts.
# It is never sent to the browser and never logged -- only the derived address is.
#
# TESTNET ONLY. Never put a mainnet key here. Never commit this value.
# The wallet needs tFIL (gas) and USDFC (storage). Calibration faucet:
#   https://faucet.calibnet.chainsafe-fil.io/
FILECOIN_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Calibration RPC endpoint. Optional: unset uses the SDK's built-in
# Calibration fallback set.
FILECOIN_RPC_URL=https://api.calibration.node.glif.io/rpc/v1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEMO TIMESCALE  (optional, default 1 = off)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Real Warm Storage pricing is ~$0.029/month for the largest cost stream a demo
# can honestly create (2 copies x 1 GiB + one data set). A 5 USDFC deposit then
# buys roughly 5,200 days of runway, so a 14-day gauge pegs at full and no
# day-based policy threshold ever fires. A judge sees a flat line.
#
# This multiplier scales the agent's POLICY THRESHOLDS and the gauge's
# GRADUATIONS by N. It NEVER touches a value read from the chain: every number
# displayed (funds, burn rate, epochs, days remaining, balances) stays the exact
# onchain reading, and the UI carries a "DEMO TIMESCALE xN - READINGS REAL" badge.
#
# Read it as: "for this demo, treat N days of runway the way a production agent
# would treat 1 day." Behaviour is real; only risk appetite is rescaled.
#
# Use the NEXT_PUBLIC_ form: the gauge is a client component and Next.js only
# inlines NEXT_PUBLIC_* into the client bundle. Setting only the server-side
# variable would make the agent act on scaled thresholds while the gauge still
# drew a 14-day axis. `npm run bootstrap -- status` prints a suggested value.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FILRUNWAY_DEMO_SCALE=1

# Server-only fallback, honoured by the policy engine, agent loop and ops CLI
# when the NEXT_PUBLIC_ form is unset. Keep both at the same value if you set it.
FILRUNWAY_DEMO_SCALE=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DECISION JOURNAL  (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An autonomous TOP_UP and `npm run bootstrap -- fund 5` are byte-identical on
# chain. The only evidence that the AGENT authored a transaction is the Decision
# that preceded it, so decisions are appended to an append-only JSONL file that
# survives a server restart. Read it with `npm run decisions`.
#
# LEAVE THIS UNSET. Unset, the path is derived per mode:
#
#   FILRUNWAY_MODE=live  ->  data/decisions.jsonl        (the real record)
#   FILRUNWAY_MODE=mock  ->  data/decisions.mock.jsonl   (simulated)
#
# so a demo run can never append simulated spend into the file that proves the
# agent's real transaction. Both are gitignored.
#
# Setting it explicitly points BOTH modes at that one file. That is still safe —
# every record is stamped MOCK or LIVE and every read is scoped to one mode, so
# the dashboard and `npm run decisions` stay separated either way — but it puts
# the two streams back in one file. Set to `off` to disable persistence and keep
# decisions in memory only, as they were before.
#
# FILRUNWAY_DECISION_LOG=data/decisions.jsonl

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEPLOYMENT  (Vercel)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything below matters only on a deployed instance. Locally, all of it is
# unset and nothing about `npm run dev` changes.
#
# HOW THE AGENT IS DRIVEN
# The local agent loop is a setInterval. A Vercel Function lives for one
# request, so there is no process to hold a timer: deployed, the cycle is driven
# by the Cron Job declared in vercel.ts, which calls GET /api/tick every minute.
# The driver is chosen from Vercel's own VERCEL=1 marker; override only if you
# self-host a long-running server on a platform that also sets it.
# FILRUNWAY_AGENT_DRIVER=interval

# Cron schedule, read at BUILD time by vercel.ts. Default "* * * * *".
# Per-minute schedules require a Pro plan; Hobby cron jobs run at most once a
# day. On Hobby, set something the plan accepts here and drive the real cadence
# from an external scheduler calling /api/tick with the bearer token below.
# FILRUNWAY_CRON_SCHEDULE=* * * * *

# What the dashboard should BELIEVE the tick cadence is, in ms. Default 60000,
# which is what the default per-minute cron schedule above actually delivers.
# Read at runtime, unlike the schedule itself, and it changes nothing about when
# a tick happens — only the NEXT TICK countdown the dashboard draws.
#
# Set it when an EXTERNAL scheduler is driving the real cadence (the Hobby-plan
# path above): a scheduler calling /api/tick every 5 minutes with the countdown
# left at 60s would show a clock that expires and then sits there lying. Match
# this to the external cadence so NEXT TICK counts down to something real.
# FILRUNWAY_CRON_INTERVAL_MS=60000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TICK AUTHENTICATION  (required on any public deployment)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /api/tick is the only endpoint that can SPEND. On a public URL with a funded
# key in the environment, an unauthenticated version of it lets anyone drain the
# wallet. It therefore requires this shared secret, compared in constant time.
#
# CRON_SECRET is Vercel's own name for it: set it on the project and Vercel Cron
# sends `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET` on every scheduled invocation, so
# the schedule authenticates itself. An operator ticks by hand with the same
# header. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# A deployment with no CRON_SECRET REFUSES every tick (503) rather than falling
# open. Set it before the first deploy. Never commit the value.
# CRON_SECRET=

# Force the check on or off regardless of the driver. Only for tests.
# FILRUNWAY_REQUIRE_TICK_AUTH=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DURABLE JOURNAL ON VERCEL  (Vercel Blob)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A Function's filesystem is read-only apart from an ephemeral /tmp, so
# data/decisions.jsonl cannot survive there — and that file is the only evidence
# that the AGENT, rather than the operator's CLI, authored a transaction. On
# Vercel the journal is written to Vercel Blob instead, as append-only JSONL
# segments, with identical MOCK/LIVE stamping and mode-scoped reads.
#
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN is injected automatically once a Blob store is connected
# to the project; you do not set it by hand. Pull it into .env.local with
# `vercel env pull .env.local` to read the deployed journal locally:
#
#   npm run decisions -- --remote
#
# Locally this is ignored even if present: the local record stays on disk, where
# every existing reference to data/decisions.jsonl expects it.
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=

# Where in the Blob store the journal lives. Default "filrunway/journal".
# FILRUNWAY_BLOB_PREFIX=filrunway/journal

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SPENDING SAFETY CAP
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An unattended agent with a funded wallet needs a limit it cannot argue with.
# Over a rolling 24h window the agent will make at most this many deposits, and
# deposit at most this much in total. Reaching either limit produces a SAFETY_CAP
# decision — recorded, with reasoning, in the journal — and NO transaction.
#
# The defaults suit the shipped rule set exactly: one emergency top-up (15 USDFC)
# plus one scheduled top-up (5 USDFC) is 20 USDFC, so the agent can answer a real
# emergency in full and must then stop for the day.
#
# Enforced in LIVE mode only, where real funds move. A MOCK run is capped by
# nothing, so the local demo is unchanged.
# FILRUNWAY_MAX_DEPOSITS_24H=3
# FILRUNWAY_MAX_DEPOSIT_USDFC_24H=20
# FILRUNWAY_SPEND_WINDOW_MS=86400000
# FILRUNWAY_SPEND_CAP=on
